{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by acid screenwriter jesse eisenberg. the film is based on the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook by harvard college students mark zuckerberg and his roommates eduardo saverin, andrew mccollum, and dustin moskovitz.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, a socially awkward but intellectually gifted student who creates a website called facemash, which becomes a huge success at harvard. however, his success attracts the attention of other students, including cambridge university student andrew garfield as eduardo saverin, who becomes his business partner and friend.\n\nas the film progresses, we see mark zuckerberg's relationships with his roommates and friends become increasingly strained, particularly with eduardo saverin, who is portrayed as a charming and sophisticated international student from brazil. mark zuckerberg's obsession with facebook grows, and he begins to neglect his relationships with his friends and family.\n\nthe film also explores the theme of class and privilege, particularly in the way that the harvard elite view thefacebook as a way to connect with other students, but also as a way to maintain their social status and connections. this is exemplified in the character of sean parker, played by josh hutcherson, who becomes a key player in the development of facebook but is also portrayed as a bit of a troublemaker.\n\none of the most striking aspects of the film is its portrayal of the characters, particularly mark zuckerberg. jesse eisenberg's performance is nuanced and complex, capturing the character's awkwardness and intensity. the film also features a strong supporting cast, including andrew garfield as eduardo saverin and josh hutcherson as sean parker.\n\nthe film's direction by david fincher is also noteworthy, capturing the frenetic energy and intensity of the harvard campus in the early 2000s. the film's cinematography is also noteworthy, with a distinctive color palette and a sense of claustrophobia that captures the intensity of the characters' emotions.\n\nthe film's script by jesse eisenberg is also highly praised, capturing the witty banter and clever dialogue that defines the characters. the film's score by trent reznor and atticus ross is also noteworthy, capturing the sense of unease and tension that defines the film.\n\noverall, the social network is a gripping and thought-provoking film that explores the complexities of friendship, class, and privilege. the film's strong performances, direction, and script make it a must-see for anyone interested in the story of facebook and its founders.\n\nrating: 9/10\n\nawards and nominations:\n\n* academy award for best picture (nomination)\n* academy award for best director (david fincher, nomination)\n* academy award for best actor (jesse eisenberg, nomination)\n* golden globe award for best motion picture - drama (nomination)\n* golden globe award for best actor - motion picture drama (jesse eisenberg, nomination)\n\nbox office:\n\n* worldwide gross: $224 million\n* domestic gross: $96 million\n* international gross: $128 million", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.045232724}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -0.0016868184}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -7.6293654e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.18040486}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.00012385078}, {"token": " bi", "logprob": -1.8251517}, {"token": "ographical", "logprob": -0.00014363688}, {"token": " drama", "logprob": -0.0063425205}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.0024567442}, {"token": " directed", "logprob": -1.0097208}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -0.0015062665}, {"token": " david", "logprob": -0.009424718}, {"token": " fin", "logprob": -0.00040570612}, {"token": "cher", "logprob": -4.005352e-05}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.9112